# DTF tier manifest: backup-reclaim  (#2787)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The backup archive is a plain object on the
# storage backend; the storage-leak class is backend-agnostic, so this runs on
# the default filesystem overlay (the archive lands on disk inside the backend
# container at /data/storage/<storage_path>, directly inspectable via
# `docker exec`).
#
# The bug (#2787): BackupService retention cleanup deleted the `backups` DB row
# but LEAKED the .tar.gz storage object (BackupService::cleanup ran a bare
# `DELETE FROM backups` and never touched storage). The fix deletes the archive
# object first, then the row.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh) is the discriminating gate:
#   * create + execute a backup -> assert the .tar.gz archive exists on the
#     storage backend AND a completed `backups` row exists
#   * age the row past retention (SQL) + set backup_retention_count=0 so a single
#     aged backup is eligible, then trigger POST /api/v1/admin/cleanup
#   * assert BOTH the `backups` row AND the .tar.gz object are gone.
#     Pre-#2787 the object remains on disk (leak) => the object-absent assertion
#     fails. On the rc (fixed) image both are gone => PASS.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives admin login + several admin API
# calls back-to-back; the base default (ON) could throttle them.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
